  isHtmlView: true,
  introduction: "Three Dimensional Geometry extends vector methods to study lines and planes in spatial coordinate frames. In this chapter, we master direction cosines and direction ratios, vector and Cartesian equations of lines in space, angles between skew and intersecting lines, shortest distance between skew and parallel lines, vector and Cartesian equations of planes, intercepts, family of planes passing through the intersection of two planes, coplanarity, and perpendicular distances from points to planes.",
  summary: [
    "Direction Cosines & Ratios: If α, β, γ are direction angles, direction cosines l = cos α, m = cos β, n = cos γ satisfy l² + m² + n² = 1. Ratios a, b, c are proportional to l, m, n.",
    "Line Equation (Point & Direction): Vector r⃗ = a⃗ + λb⃗, Cartesian (x - x₁)/a = (y - y₁)/b = (z - z₁)/c.",
    "Line Equation (Two Points): Vector r⃗ = a⃗ + λ(b⃗ - a⃗), Cartesian (x - x₁)/(x₂ - x₁) = (y - y₁)/(y₂ - y₁) = (z - z₁)/(z₂ - z₁).",
    "Angle Between Two Lines: cos θ = |a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ + c₁c₂| / [√(a₁² + b₁² + c₁²) √(a₂² + b₂² + c₂²)]. Perpendicular if a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ + c₁c₂ = 0; Parallel if a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ = c₁/c₂.",
    "Shortest Distance Between Skew Lines: d = |(b₁⃗ × b₂⃗) · (a₂⃗ - a₁⃗)| / |b₁⃗ × b₂⃗|.",
    "Plane Equation: Normal form r⃗ · n̂ = d (lx + my + nz = d); Point-normal form (r⃗ - a⃗) · N⃗ = 0 (A(x - x₁) + B(y - y₁) + C(z - z₁) = 0); Intercept form x/a + y/b + z/c = 1.",
    "Plane Family & Distances: Plane through intersection is P₁ + λP₂ = 0. Perpendicular distance from (x₁, y₁, z₁) to plane Ax + By + Cz + D = 0 is d = |Ax₁ + By₁ + Cz₁ + D| / √(A² + B² + C²)."
],
  definitions: [
    {
        "term": "Direction Cosines",
        "definition": "The cosines of the angles α, β, γ that a directed ray makes with positive x, y, and z axes: l = cos α, m = cos β, n = cos γ, satisfying l² + m² + n² = 1."
    },
    {
        "term": "Direction Ratios",
        "definition": "Any three numbers a, b, c proportional to the direction cosines l, m, n of a directed line in three-dimensional space."
    },
    {
        "term": "Skew Lines",
        "definition": "Lines in three-dimensional space that are neither parallel nor intersecting, lying in different non-parallel planes."
    },
    {
        "term": "Normal to a Plane",
        "definition": "A vector perpendicular to every straight line lying in the plane, determining the plane's spatial inclination."
    }
],
